# Contributing to `<%= name %>`

Thank you for considering contributing to `<%= name %>`!

## Prerequisites

Either **Nix** -- `nix develop` gives Ruby and every tool below at the versions `flake.lock` pins
-- or the tools installed yourself. The gems are Bundler's either way, from `Gemfile.lock`, as in
CI (nixpkgs packages few of them, and older).

- **[Ruby](https://www.ruby-lang.org) 4.0** or later, with Bundler (Ruby's own). `bin/setup`
  installs the gems `Gemfile.lock` pins: Rake, RuboCop, its extensions and rubydex (its project
  index), Steep, TypeProf, Minitest, SimpleCov, pbt, mutineer, YARD, yard-lint, bundler-audit, IRB.
- **[ShellCheck](https://www.shellcheck.net/)** and **[shfmt](https://github.com/mvdan/sh)** --
  the shell's linter and formatter (its style is `.editorconfig`'s).
- **[actionlint](https://github.com/rhysd/actionlint)** -- lints the workflows (and, through
  ShellCheck, their `run:` scripts).
- **[zizmor](https://docs.zizmor.sh/)** -- audits the workflows' security, and Dependabot's
  configuration.
- **[dprint](https://dprint.dev/)** -- formats the Markdown and TOML.
- **[cocogitto](https://docs.cocogitto.io/)** (`cog`) -- enforces Conventional Commits.
- **[git-cliff](https://git-cliff.org/)** -- writes the changelog at a release.
- **[lychee](https://lychee.cli.rs/)** -- checks the links of the files git tracks, online
  (`rake links`; `links.yml` monthly too).
- **[Nix](https://nixos.org)** for the last of `rake checks`, the flake's checks (`rake nix`):
  without it, `rake checks` stops there, every other check passed.

## Setup

1. Fork this repository and create your branch from `main`.
2. Clone your fork, and install the gems:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/<you>/<%= name %> && cd <%= name %>
bin/setup
```

3. Optionally, `bundle exec rake hooks`: `.githooks/` then runs RuboCop, the shell, the workflows
   and dprint before each commit, checks its message as a Conventional Commit, and every pushed
   commit's message before a push: what CI would refuse, minutes earlier.
   `git config --unset core.hooksPath` turns them off.

## Guidelines

Before you start, read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md): the lint and type policy, and what to do when a
cop or a diagnostic fires. It applies to human and AI-assisted contributions alike.

## Testing

- **Pure functions:** isolate logic from side effects wherever possible and cover it with unit
  tests (Minitest, `test/test_*.rb`).
- **Properties:** where a rule holds for every input, state it as a property (pbt,
  `Pbt.assert { Pbt.property(...) { ... } }`): it is checked on many generated inputs, and a
  failure is shrunk to its smallest counterexample, with the seed that reproduces it.
- **Regressions:** if you are fixing a bug, include a test that reproduces the previous failure.
- **Coverage:** every line, branch and method of `lib/` (SimpleCov's `strict` profile,
  configured in `.simplecov`, started by `rake test`). Code a test cannot reach is questioned
  before it is excluded. Some tests (`bundle exec rake test N=/version/`) run fewer than the gate
  counts: their exit fails, the tests' own verdict above it. One file run alone
  (`bundle exec ruby -Ilib -Itest test/test_<%= underscored %>.rb`) is not measured.
- **Warnings:** one from `lib/` or the tests fails them, every category of Ruby's on
  (`test/test_helper.rb`); a gem's only prints.
- **Mutations:** `bundle exec rake mutation` changes `lib/` a mutant at a time (mutineer,
  `.mutineer.yml`) and lists each one no test catches: a test missing, or a change that keeps the
  behaviour, which `.mutineer.yml`'s `ignore:` holds with its reason. One survivor fails it
  (`threshold: 100`). It takes minutes: `rake checks` mutates the lines changed since `origin/main`
  alone (`rake mutation:changed`; all of them where there is none), and `ruby-ci.yml` every line.

## Development Workflow

1. Every commit must be a [Conventional Commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) (`cog check`
   enforces it), and its subject is its changelog entry (git-cliff writes the changelog at each
   release): write it for the gem's users.
2. Run the checks. The Rakefile runs the workflows' commands:

```sh
bundle exec rake          # RuboCop, Steep, the tests, yard-lint: ruby-ci.yml
bundle exec rake checks   # and bundler-audit, dprint, cog, the shell, the workflows, the links,
                          # the changed lines' mutations, the flake's checks
bundle exec rake format   # RuboCop's corrections, dprint and nixfmt, in place
bundle exec rake typeprof # the signatures TypeProf infers from lib/, to start sig/ from
bundle exec rake yard     # the documentation of lib/, in doc/
bundle exec rake graph    # its classes and modules, for Graphviz: | dot -Tsvg -o graph.svg
nix build                 # the gem, built and installed by Nix (flake.nix)
```

`bundle exec rake -T` lists every task, and `bundle exec rake -D` describes each in full.

A new dependency of the gem goes in the gemspec (`spec.add_dependency`), and, for the Nix package,
its nixpkgs package in flake.nix's `dependencies` (`pkgs.rubyPackages_4_0.<gem>`): the flake's
`library` check, which loads the gem with its dependencies, fails without it, or with a version
the gemspec's requirement excludes -- one nixpkgs lacks is built there by `pkgs.buildRubyGem`. A
new tool goes in the Gemfile, which the built gem never holds: its dependencies are the gemspec's
alone. `bundle install` then updates the lock. Steep reads a library's signatures when the
Steepfile names it (`library "json"`): the standard library's, and a gem's own (its `sig/`). A gem
that ships none may have them in gem_rbs_collection, read through an RBS collection
(`rbs collection init`, then `rbs collection install`, as rbs's `docs/collection.md` describes).

## Creating a Pull Request

1. Make sure `bundle exec rake checks` passes.
2. Open a pull request against `main`, titled as a conventional commit: a squash merge of two or
   more commits makes the title its commit, and so a changelog entry (CI checks it). Describe the
   problem it solves, and link its issue (`Fixes #123`), if any.
3. Wait for a maintainer's review.

## Maintainers

### First steps

`bundle veragem` made the git repository and added its files. The lock is yours to write, from
this machine, and then the first commit -- which `cog check` (run by `rake checks` and by CI)
requires to follow [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/):

```sh
bin/setup
git add .
git commit -m "chore: initial commit from veragem"
```

With [Nix](https://nixos.org), `nix develop` first: its Ruby and tools are the ones `flake.lock`
pins. A flake sees git's files only, so they are added already.

Then, in whichever order suits you:

- Push to `https://github.com/<%= github %>/<%= name %>`.
- Enable the repository settings the auto-merge workflow needs, listed at the top of
  `.github/workflows/dependabot-automerge.yml`: without required status checks, it merges bumps
  that CI never gated.
- Enable private vulnerability reporting (Settings → Advanced Security; a public repository's
  alone): it is off by default, and [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) and the issue form point to it.
- Enable the dependency graph (Settings → Advanced Security), if it is off: the dependency review
  (`dependency-review.yml`) fails without it. A private repository's needs GitHub Code Security
  too, an organization's; without it, delete the workflow.
- Add a pending trusted publisher on [RubyGems](https://rubygems.org/profile/oidc/pending_trusted_publishers)
  for the gem `<%= name %>`: this repository, the workflow `release.yml`, the environment
  `release` (a publisher without one admits any of the workflow's jobs, its signing job's too). A
  release tag then publishes without a stored key, its first version too, and its GitHub release
  follows RubyGems': without the publisher, neither is made.
- Limit the `release` environment's deployments to the release tags (Settings → Environments, a
  rule of ref type Tag, `v*`): a trusted publisher names the environment, not a ref, so without the
  rule a workflow pushed to any branch could publish the gem. And limit who creates the release tags
  (Settings → Rules → Rulesets, a tag ruleset on `v*`): a tag pushed on any commit runs that
  commit's workflows in the environment.
<%- if exe -%>
- After the first release, make the image public: GHCR publishes a new package as private (its
  page, Package settings → Change visibility).
- Limit the `image` environment's deployments to the release tags as well (a rule of ref type Tag,
  `v*`): its jobs push the image.
- To publish the image to Docker Hub too: create its repository there, then set the repository
  variable `DOCKERHUB_USERNAME` and the `image` environment's secret `DOCKERHUB_TOKEN` (an access
  token that writes; only the environment's jobs, on a release tag, can read it) -- and the variable
  `DOCKERHUB_REPOSITORY` (`namespace/name`) unless it is `<username>/<%= name %>`. A Docker Hub
  failure in a push (its login, its name) holds GHCR's tag back too.
<%- end -%>
<%- if citation -%>
- To archive each release with a DOI, enable the repository on [Zenodo](https://zenodo.org) (its
  profile menu → GitHub, the repository's toggle): a GitHub release, `release.yml`'s, is then
  archived, its metadata `CITATION.cff`'s. After the first, uncomment the DOIs at the end of
  `CITATION.cff` and set them: the concept DOI, which names every version (Zenodo's "Cite all
  versions") and which GitHub's citation then links, and a release's own, which Zenodo mints only
  after its tag, so the file can name an earlier version's alone.
<%- unless licenses.one? -%>
  Zenodo's import refuses `CITATION.cff`'s list of licences, the Citation File Format's OR, so
  it archives no release until
  [zenodo/zenodo-rdm#1509](https://github.com/zenodo/zenodo-rdm/issues/1509) is fixed.
<%- end -%>
<%- end -%>
- Run `bundle exec rake checks` once to confirm your tools are complete: a step that stops on a
  missing command names it ([Prerequisites](#prerequisites) lists them; `nix develop` has them
  all).
- `bundle exec rake hooks`, if you want git to run the quick checks before each commit and push
  ([Setup](#setup)).
- Make the project yours: the code, the tests, the signatures, the README and the gemspec's
  summary.

### Releasing

From a clean `main`, CI green on it:

```sh
bundle exec rake changelog          # the changes the next release will list
bundle exec rake "bump[minor]"      # or major, or patch
bundle exec rake release
```

`rake bump` refuses a release with nothing in it: no commit since the last tag, merges and fixups
aside, which the changelog leaves out. It sets the version in `lib/<%= path %>/version.rb` (the
first release keeps the version declared, whatever the level), updates the lock, writes the
changelog (git-cliff) and commits.
<%- if citation -%>
It sets `CITATION.cff`'s version and date-released too: a citation names the release.
<%- end -%>
`rake release`, Bundler's own, builds the gem, tags `v<version>` and pushes the branch and the
tag; it pushes no gem. The tag runs `release.yml`: the Ruby checks (`rake`), then the build
(`gem build`, no bundle loaded) and its Sigstore attestation, the push to RubyGems by Trusted
Publishing, which shows the gem's provenance, then the GitHub release of the same gem, its notes
the changelog's new section.
<%- if exe -%>
Beside `release.yml`, waiting for no RubyGems push, `image.yml` publishes the command's OCI image
to GHCR (`nix build .#image` builds it here).
<%- end -%>

`rake release` pushes to `main` directly: with "Require a pull request before merging" on, the
maintainer cutting it needs the rule's bypass. A refused push leaves the release commit and
removes the tag (Bundler's doing): run `rake release` again once allowed.

## Updating the tools

Dependabot moves the gems (`Gemfile.lock`) and the actions weekly, and the flake's inputs
monthly; `dependabot-automerge.yml` merges the minor and patch bumps once the checks pass. An
advisory with no fixed version yet fails `security.yml` on every pull request until you weigh it:
its ID under `ignore:` in `.bundler-audit.yml` (bundler-audit's own file) lets the checks pass.
dprint's plugins, pinned in `dprint.json`, move by hand: `dprint config update`. A new RuboCop
brings new cops (`NewCops: enable`), a new Steep new diagnostics: read them before silencing any.
A bump the new cops fail stays unmerged: its failed run's summary holds RuboCop's safe corrections
as a patch, to `git apply` on its branch (no workflow pushes code the bumped gems wrote); what
they leave is yours to fix.
